"The client spending cuts in the retail and manufacturing sectors, facing challenges from supply chain disruptions and reduced consumer spending, have further impacted the revenue prospects of Indian IT companies.",1
"The currency fluctuations, particularly the strengthening of the US dollar against the Indian rupee, have impacted the profitability of Indian IT companies, as a significant portion of their revenue is generated from overseas markets.",1
"The impact of a strong dollar, while potentially beneficial for export revenues, can also lead to increased volatility and uncertainty in earnings, impacting investor confidence.",1
"The increased competition from global and local players, particularly in emerging technologies like cloud computing, artificial intelligence, and cybersecurity, has placed pressure on the pricing and profitability of Indian IT companies.6",1
"The competition in cloud computing, from global giants like Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud, has intensified, requiring Indian IT companies to invest heavily in developing cloud capabilities.",1
"The competition in artificial intelligence and cybersecurity, from both global and local players, has necessitated significant investments in research and development to maintain a competitive edge.",1
"The investor sentiment and market correction, characterized by profit booking and portfolio adjustments, have further contributed to the decline in Indian IT stock prices.",1
"The profit booking by investors, seeking to capitalize on gains accumulated during previous periods of growth, has exerted downward pressure on share prices.7",1
"The portfolio adjustments by institutional investors, seeking to rebalance their holdings in response to changing market conditions, have further impacted the demand for Indian IT stocks.",1
