"The dense population, exceeding 20 million residents, creates intense competition for available land, further driving up prices and limiting the size of potential acquisitions.",1
"The limited land availability, due to environmental restrictions, coastal regulations, and existing urban development, creates a highly competitive market, where even small plots of land are highly sought after.",1
"The zoning regulations in Mumbai, encompassing residential, commercial, and industrial zones, along with floor space index (FSI) restrictions, significantly impact the development potential and pricing of land parcels.",1
"The residential zoning, characterized by varying FSI allowances and building height restrictions, dictates the type and density of residential development permitted on a given plot of land.",1
"The commercial zoning, encompassing areas designated for office buildings, retail spaces, and commercial establishments, typically commands higher land values due to its development potential.",1
"The industrial zoning, located primarily in the outskirts of the city, offers relatively larger land parcels but may be subject to environmental regulations and development restrictions.",1
